  "notes": "Murder Inc.'s sped-up 80s R&B, and the sharp key set is the parameter, not a decoration: the research says the interpolations are sped up \"so keys read sharp\", so `session.keys` is the sharp side — E, B, F#, A, D and their relative minors — where every other entry on this axis sits in the flat-side minor pool the genre inherits. The second parameter is the kick: 1 and **3**, not 1 and 3.5, which is the disco-adjacent pull that separates his kit from the boom-bap norm. The third is the counter-hook — \"a sung female counter-hook is a structural fixture\" — so `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.65 with `fillGapsOnly` off, which is the highest on this axis. ⚠ `medium` and the research says medium as well: the method is documented journalistically rather than technically, so every drum probability here is inference from prose. The specific inferred parameter is `drums.hihat.fillDensity` — \"hats 8ths plus 16th shuffles\" gives a vocabulary but no rate. ⚠ The I–vi–IV–V family is INFERRED; the research names I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V, and it carries the lowest weight. ⚠ `countermelody.fillGapsOnly` returned to true. With it off and a density of 0.65, the counter plays THROUGH the melody rather than around it, and coherence.rs measures that as the counter sounding the melody own pitch at the same moment — a second lead rather than an answer. The register offset and the styles are untouched; what changed is that it waits its turn.",
